<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <application
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/AppTheme">

        <activity
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|locale|smallestScreenSize|screenLayout|uiMode|navigation|density"
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:label="@string/title_activity_main"
            android:theme="@style/AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:exported="true">

            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

        </activity>

        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.fileprovider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/file_paths"></meta-data>
        </provider>
    </application>

    <!-- Permissions -->

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!--
      B02 (2026-09-29) · THE MICROPHONE, WHICH IS MOST OF THE PRODUCT.

      Dex is spoken to: the Desk's ripple, the capture pop-up, the onboarding
      interview, a note on a task. In the APK every one of them answered
      "Microphone not available" the instant it was pressed, and the app never
      appeared under Settings -> Apps -> DecisionOS -> Permissions at all.

      Nothing in the web app was wrong. A webview's getUserMedia arrives at
      Capacitor's BridgeWebChromeClient.onPermissionRequest, which maps
      AUDIO_CAPTURE onto the Android runtime permissions below and launches the
      system prompt. A runtime permission that is not DECLARED here cannot be
      requested: the launcher returns denied immediately, without ever showing
      a dialog, and Capacitor then calls request.deny(). So the founder saw an
      error with no prompt, and no way to fix it in system settings either.

      MODIFY_AUDIO_SETTINGS goes with it because Capacitor asks for both
      together; it is not a runtime permission and raises no prompt of its own.

      Camera is deliberately NOT here. Nothing in the app opens a camera stream
      today — bills are attached through the file picker — and an undeclared
      permission is one the store does not have to be told about.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.MODIFY_AUDIO_SETTINGS" />
</manifest>
